<Base
  title="Why now"
  description="The measured case that this is a moment for constitutional reform: the appetite for structural change, the symmetric experience behind it, and the movement already under way."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Why now</h1>

    <p>
      The Constitution has not been amended since 1992 — and the amendment
      ratified that year, the Twenty-seventh, was an anti-self-dealing rule for
      Congress, proposed two centuries earlier (<a
        href="https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/amendments-11-27"
        >National Archives</a
      >). A project that proposes new amendments owes an answer to the obvious
      question: why would this moment be different? Our answer is not a
      prediction. It is a set of measurements.
    </p>

    <h2>The appetite, measured</h2>
    <p>
      In Pew Research Center's spring 2025 survey, 77% of Americans said the
      nation's political system needs major changes or needs to be completely
      reformed — but only 27% both want major change and are confident it can
      happen. Pew calls the 49% in between "pessimistic reformers": people who
      want the system changed and do not believe it will be. Pew publishes no
      party breakdown for these whole-system questions (<a
        href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2026/04/15/americans-stand-out-internationally-for-their-pessimism-about-the-nations-political-system/"
        >Pew's April 2026 analysis of the survey</a
      >). The United States has the highest GDP per capita of the 25 countries
      Pew surveyed, and reports reform sentiment at the level of middle-income
      countries.
    </p>
    <p>
      When Pew itself rounded up the specific changes Americans favor, it
      titled the piece "Most Americans favor big changes to the political
      system that likely would require amending the Constitution" — and those
      numbers do carry party breakdowns: congressional term limits at 87% (90%
      of Republicans, 86% of Democrats, 2023), age limits for federal officials
      at 79% (82% R, 76% D, 2023), limits on campaign spending at 72% (71% R,
      76% D, 2023) (<a
        href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2026/07/28/most-americans-favor-big-changes-to-the-political-system-that-likely-would-require-amending-the-constitution/"
        >Pew, July 2026</a
      >).
    </p>

    <h2>Both sides have now been on both ends</h2>
    <p>
      Over the past decade each party has held the presidency, and each has
      watched powers it once defended used in ways it opposed. That experience
      is visible in the polling record itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Partnership for Public Service asked an identical question — whether
      a nonpartisan civil service is important for a strong American democracy
      — in three consecutive springs. Support ran 87% in 2024, fell to 66% in
      2025, and recovered to 76% in 2026; among Republicans, roughly 87%, then
      66%, then 78% (<a
        href="https://ourpublicservice.org/publications/the-state-of-public-trust-in-government-2024/"
        >2024</a
      >,
      <a
        href="https://ourpublicservice.org/publications/the-state-of-public-trust-in-government-2025/"
        >2025</a
      >,
      <a
        href="https://ourpublicservice.org/know-the-facts/resource-library/reports/steady-opposition-public-disapproval-of-trump-administration-funding-and-workforce-cuts-remains-strong"
        >2026</a
      >). A twenty-one-point fall and a twelve-point recovery, on a principle,
      in twenty-four months. On pardons, when YouGov asked in early 2025
      whether it is acceptable for a president to pardon family members, 72%
      of Republicans and 42% of Democrats said unacceptable — each party's
      answer bending to excuse the power its own party's president had
      exercised (<a
        href="https://yougov.com/en-us/articles/51548-where-americans-draw-the-line-on-presidential-behavior"
        >YouGov, 2025</a
      >); we have measured the same collapse running the other way, on other
      pardon questions, as the presidency changed hands. On redistricting, a
      question naming both Texas and California found
      71% opposed to mid-decade redraws (70% R, 73% D) — and between two
      survey waves in late 2025, as the second state joined, Republican
      opposition rose eight points while Democratic opposition fell seven (<a
        href="https://law.marquette.edu/poll/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/MLSPSC29PressReleaseNationalIssues.pdf"
        >Marquette, November 2025</a
      >).
    </p>
    <blockquote class="veil">
      <p>
        Rules written behind a veil of ignorance — binding all future
        presidents and all future majorities equally — can now be weighed by
        both sides with the same fear in mind. No one knows who a fair rule
        will bind next.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>The movement is already under way</h2>
    <p>
      The timing case is not only in surveys. Twenty-five states have formally
      called on Congress to propose a money-in-politics amendment, the two
      most recent in spring 2026, with Missouri's House passing its resolution
      unanimously (<a
        href="https://www.democracyrenovator.com/p/25-states-back-amendment-to-limit"
        >Democracy Renovator</a
      >;
      <a
        href="https://thebeaconnews.org/stories/2026/02/26/missouri-bill-to-overturn-citizens-united/"
        >The Beacon, February 2026</a
      >), and in June 2026 that amendment got its first Republican-introduced
      congressional version (<a
        href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/191/text"
        >H.J.Res. 191</a
      >). U.S. Term Limits reports that fourteen states have passed its
      Article V application — an advocacy-sourced count — with the
      congressional text pending as
      <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/12/text"
        >H.J.Res. 12</a
      >
      and S.J.Res. 1 (<a href="https://termlimits.com/progress/"
        >U.S. Term Limits</a
      >). And in July 2026 the House passed a ban on foreign contributions to
      ballot-measure campaigns by voice vote (<a
        href="https://news.ballotpedia.org/2026/07/18/u-s-house-passes-bill-banning-foreign-contributions-to-state-and-local-ballot-measure-campaigns/"
        >Ballotpedia</a
      >).
    </p>

    <h2>What we make of it — and what we don't</h2>
    <p>
      Some of today's reform appetite may fade with the next change of
      administration. Our own research shows the pattern: several of the
      numbers above moved with which party held the presidency, and a support
      floor that tracks the White House is grievance, not consensus. So we
      measure for exactly that, and we build only
      on the numbers that held through changes of administration: term limits,
      the ethics rules, campaign spending limits, the symmetric redistricting
      rule, the lobbying waits.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not predict ratification. What we can say is what we have
      measured: half the country wants structural change and does not believe
      it can happen. The evidence that Americans across the spectrum already
      agree on specific, durable changes — published with party breakdowns,
      alongside everything that failed the test — is our answer to that
      disbelief.
    </p>

    <p class="next">
      <a href="/amendments">Read the amendments →</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

